<section>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Using a uniform ladder of mass <i>m</i> = 10 kg and length <i>ℓ</i> = 4 m,
    at θ = 60° to the ground, with <i>g</i> = 9.81 m/s²:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Vertical: <i>N</i> = 10 × 9.81 = 98.10 N</li>
    <li>Moments about the foot: <i>S</i> × 4 sin 60° = 98.10 × 2 cos 60°</li>
    <li>4 × 0.8660 <i>S</i> = 98.10 × 1, so 3.464 <i>S</i> = 98.10</li>
    <li><i>S</i> = 28.32 N, and <i>F</i> = <i>S</i> = 28.32 N</li>
    <li>Least <i>μ</i> = <i>F</i>/<i>N</i> = 28.32 / 98.10 = 0.289</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    These match the live values at the default mass and angle. With the
    default <i>μ</i> = 0.40, which is greater than 0.289, the status is
    EQUILIBRIUM.
  </p>
  <p>
    Drop <i>μ</i> to 0.20. Then 0.20 &lt; 0.289, so friction cannot reach
    28.32 N and the status becomes WOULD SLIP. The required <i>F</i> does
    not change — only whether the ground can supply it.
  </p>
</section>
